  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Average Signal Power",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute average signal power for a continuous-time signal by evaluating the symmetric long-time average of $|x(t)|^2$ and interpreting the value as sustained signal size distinct from finite total energy.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Average Signal Power",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Average Signal Power",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Average signal power is the long-run measure of sustained signal size. For a continuous-time signal $x(t)$, we define it by averaging the squared magnitude over a symmetric window that grows without bound.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The symmetric interval $[-T,T]$ matters because the average should reflect long-time behavior on both sides of the reference time, not accumulation over a finite window. Squaring $|x(t)|$ makes the quantity nonnegative and measures size rather than sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the constant-signal base case $x(t)=A$, the squared magnitude is the same at every time, so the average does not change with the window length. That makes the result easy to read: a steady nonzero signal has average power $|A|^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = |A|^2 \\quad \\text{when } x(t)=A",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is why average power is different from total energy: it describes sustained size, not total accumulated area. Signals that only exist for a finite time or decay to zero will later collapse to zero average power, while steady periodic signals can keep a nonzero average power.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Average Signal Power",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing Average Power of a Constant Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the average signal power of the constant signal $x(t)=A$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is that $x(t)$ never changes with time. That means $|x(t)|^2$ is also constant, so the average-power definition should collapse to that same constant value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the constant signal $x(t)=A$. Then $|x(t)|^2=|A|^2$, and because that quantity does not depend on $t$, it can be pulled through the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}|A|^2\\int_{-T}^{T}dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The remaining integral is just the length of the interval $[-T,T]$, which is $2T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}|A|^2(2T) = |A|^2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the average signal power of a constant signal is the squared magnitude of the constant: $P_{\\text{avg}}=|A|^2$. This is the simplest example of a sustained signal whose power stays nonzero over time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Average Signal Power",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Computing Zero Average Power for a Finite-Duration Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the average signal power of the finite-duration pulse $x(t)=A$ for $|t|\\le T_0$ and $x(t)=0$ otherwise.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is finite support: this pulse is nonzero only on $[-T_0,T_0]$. That means the numerator in the average-power formula stays fixed while the averaging window keeps growing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For any $T>T_0$, the signal is zero outside $[-T_0,T_0]$, so only the finite support interval contributes to the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T_0}^{T_0}|A|^2\\,dt = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{2T_0|A|^2}{2T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The numerator is a fixed finite number, but the denominator grows without bound. So the long-time average is driven to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the key contrast with a constant signal: a finite-duration pulse can have finite energy, but it has zero average power because its nonzero part gets diluted by the growing window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Average Signal Power",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Computing Average Power of a Decaying Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the average signal power of the decaying signal $x(t)=e^{-a|t|}$ with $a>0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the even decaying envelope. Since $x(t)=e^{-a|t|}$, the squared magnitude is $|x(t)|^2=e^{-2a|t|}$, which is still symmetric about $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}e^{-2a|t|}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use even symmetry to fold the integral onto $[0,T]$ and double it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\cdot 2\\int_{0}^{T}e^{-2at}\\,dt = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{T}\\int_{0}^{T}e^{-2at}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now evaluate the exponential integral exactly. The result stays bounded while the averaging window length keeps growing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{T}\\cdot\\frac{1-e^{-2aT}}{2a} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1-e^{-2aT}}{2aT} = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the decaying signal has zero average power. Its squared magnitude is integrable, but the long-time average still vanishes because the fixed numerator is divided by an unbounded window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Average Signal Power",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Distinguishing Average Power from Total Energy",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the signal $x(t)=e^{-a|t|}$ with $a>0$, compute its total energy and its average signal power, then state which quantity is finite.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the same even decaying signal, but now the target is a comparison. Compute the total-energy integral first, then apply the long-time average to the same $|x(t)|^2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "E = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|^2\\,dt = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-2a|t|}\\,dt = 2\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-2at}\\,dt = \\frac{1}{a}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The energy integral converges, so the total energy is finite.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "P_{\\text{avg}} = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\int_{-T}^{T}e^{-2a|t|}\\,dt = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1}{2T}\\cdot 2\\int_{0}^{T}e^{-2at}\\,dt = \\lim_{T \\to \\infty}\\frac{1-e^{-2aT}}{2aT} = 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the finite quantity is total energy, not average power. This signal has bounded total accumulation, but its long-time average power is zero because the fixed energy is spread over an unbounded window.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
